<p>In the Year of Our League Two Thousand Twenty-Four, the ten men struck their
old banner and raised a new one. The <em>Whiny Little Bitch League</em> — which
had endured, gloriously misnamed, since the founding — was retired at last, and
in its place rose <em>Make Football Great Again</em>. New flag, same ten
degenerates, same $400 pressed into the pot. And as the first order of business
under the new republic, the league produced its purest miscarriage of justice in
years and handed the regular-season crown to a man whose teams had no business
wearing it.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Fraud Upon the Throne</h3>

<p><strong>David</strong> — sailing under the flag <em>Cream Hunt</em> — finished
<span class="stat">11–4</span>, first in the standings, and walked off with
<span class="stat">$950</span>, the fattest purse in the league. A triumph, if
you don't look too hard. So let us look hard. Scored against every other roster
each week — the all-play, the only honest measure of who actually played well —
David was a <span class="stat">.593</span> team dressed in a <span class="stat">.733</span>
record. He won <em>fourteen percentage points more often than he deserved to</em>,
the widest gap between merit and result in the entire league. The man built a
regular-season championship out of favorable matchups and a <span class="stat">five-game</span>
win streak against whoever the schedule happened to feed him, and then — because
the football gods keep a ledger too — he took his 1-seed into the playoffs and
finished <em>fourth</em>. And when the motion came around, as it does every
autumn, to raise the buy-in again, the German objected, as is tradition. He
objects to the buy-in the way other men object to sunrise: annually, loudly, and
to no effect.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Beneficiaries of Providence</h3>

<p>If David was the fraud, <strong>Justin</strong> was merely blessed.
<em>Rolling with Mahomies</em> backed into <span class="stat">10–5</span> and
<span class="stat">$600</span> on the second-luckiest ledger in the league — a
<span class="stat">.570</span> all-play team that the schedule kindly rounded up
to a <span class="stat">.667</span> record. He then had the decency to finish
third in the actual playoffs, which is more than the 1-seed managed. Meanwhile
<strong>Marian</strong> — <em>Olave Garden</em> — quietly scored more points than
any team in the league (<span class="stat">1,838.84</span>), dropped the season's
single biggest week in Week 2 (<span class="stat">166.18</span>, a number that
should have come with a warning label), and still couldn't finish better than
runner-up for the <span class="stat">$550</span> — outscoring the entire field and
losing the one game that mattered, which for the German is less a tragedy than a
lifestyle. It should be said that this is a man whose lone championship still
wears an asterisk the size of a dinner plate; a clean ring continues to elude
him. He made the dance by the thinnest margin imaginable, which brings us to the
man he stepped over to get there.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Commissioner's Own Purgatory</h3>

<p>Let the record show, because the commissioner's own history page cannot go
easy on the commissioner: <strong>Cory</strong>, proprietor of the team named —
without a flicker of self-awareness — <em>Zero Mistakes Were Made</em>, scored
the <em>second-most points in the entire league</em> (<span class="stat">1,830.64</span>)
and did not make the playoffs. He finished <span class="stat">9–6</span>, tied with
Marian to the win, and lost the final playoff berth on the points tiebreaker by
<span class="stat">8.20</span> — a rounding error stretched across fifteen weeks.
Eight points. Over four months. And the cruelest part, the part that belongs
framed on a wall: across that regular season he left <span class="stat">297</span>
points rotting on his own bench — the difference between the lineups he set and
the lineups he owned. He was <span class="stat">86%</span> efficient with his
roster, same as he is every single year, like a clock that is reliably eight
points slow. Set the optimal lineup even a handful of those weeks and he's in the
bracket instead of Marian. Zero mistakes were made. Two hundred and ninety-seven
of them, actually. Then, so the year could not be accused of subtlety, he lost a
Week 15 weekly-high — a clean $100 — by <span class="stat">1.06 points</span>. The
tavern will remember him by the team name.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Robbed and the Wretched</h3>

<p>At the other end of the honest ledger sat <strong>Bates</strong>, whose
<em>Kareem 🥧</em> went <span class="stat">4–11</span> and earned
<span class="stat">$0</span>, and who has a genuine grievance buried in there: by
the all-play he was a <span class="stat">.393</span> team, good for a couple more
wins than the schedule allowed, the most robbed man in the league by the
instrument. A grievance, yes. Sympathy, no — this is the same Bates the league
has spent years calling a Chiefs homer, and the receipts refuse to cooperate.
Across three drafted seasons he spent all of <em>three</em> non-keeper picks on
Kansas City, less than the German did, less than Marian did. The most notorious
Chiefs fan in the league is out-Chiefed by two Europeans who have never professed
to care about Missouri in their lives. Rib him for the reputation, then, since the
reach isn't there to rib.</p>

<p>And then there was <strong>Sam</strong>. <em>Don of a new age</em> was the
wooden spoon made flesh: <span class="stat">3–12</span>, dead last, the league's
lowest points scored (<span class="stat">1,493</span>) and — the sign of a truly
accursed campaign — its <em>highest</em> points allowed (<span class="stat">1,857</span>),
which is the schedule spitting on a man while he's down. He authored the only
sub-70 abomination of the year, a <span class="stat">67.98</span> in Week 7, and
in Week 5 he stood still while Cory hung <span class="stat">154.26</span> on him
and won by <span class="stat">75.08</span>, the biggest beating anyone
administered all season. Somebody has to buy the last-place plaque. Sam earned it
outright.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Absurdities, Duly Recorded</h3>

<p>The box scores gave up their usual crop of small cruelties. In the very first
week, before anyone had settled in, <strong>Jeremy</strong> missed the weekly-high
by <span class="stat">0.12</span> — twelve hundredths of a point — and it will be
some consolation to the doctor that the man who nipped him, Richard, was owed a
far larger reckoning the following year. <strong>Richard</strong> himself —
<em>Huggy Bear</em>, <span class="stat">7–8</span> — produced the tightest finish
of the season, edging Jeremy by <span class="stat">1.10</span> in Week 7; enjoy it,
Richard, because 2025 was coming for you. <strong>Dylan</strong>, who named his
team <em>Schmelley</em> after himself and then went <span class="stat">6–9</span>,
strung together the longest losing streak in the league — <span class="stat">six</span>
straight — a man so committed to not getting over the hump he built a monument to
it. And <strong>Michael</strong>, the reigning champion, the dynasty itself, spent
2024 proving that a dynasty can take a gap year: <em>Dixie Wrecked</em> went
<span class="stat">6–9</span> and missed the playoffs entirely despite the
second-best roster efficiency in the league and despite Josh Allen detonating for
<span class="stat">57.88</span> in Week 14 — the single best player-game anyone
got all year. He had the points. He simply spread them across the wrong weeks,
which is the dynasty's version of slumming it.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Champion</h3>

<div class="respect">
  <p class="crown">The One Paragraph of Respect</p>
  <p><strong>Jeremy</strong> — <em>Joe Momma</em> — won the whole thing, and unlike
  the man who took the regular season, he earned every inch of it. He finished
  <span class="stat">10–5</span> with the <em>best all-play record in the league</em>
  (<span class="stat">88–47</span>) and the <em>best roster efficiency in the
  league</em> (<span class="stat">91.8%</span>) — no gift matchups, no schedule
  charity, just the plain fact that week after week he fielded a better team than
  almost everyone he faced and wrung more out of it than anyone. He took the
  2-seed and closed it out for the ring and the <span class="stat">$900</span>.
  The league mocks the doctor for being a doctor; in 2024 the doctor diagnosed the
  field and operated. That one's clean. He can have it.</p>
</div>

<p>And then, because no man escapes this chronicle un-ribbed, we note for the file
that in a Week 15 loss the newly minted champion left <strong>Jared Goff and his
<span class="stat">51.06</span> points</strong> sitting on his bench — the bench
catastrophe of the year, authored by the best manager in the league, which is
exactly the sort of thing that keeps a title from going to a man's head.</p>

<p>So ended the first season under the new flag: the fraud crowned in the regular
season and dethroned in the playoffs, the doctor left holding the actual trophy,
the commissioner left holding 297 bench points and a team name he will never live
down. As ever, the final word belongs not to the standings but to the money — the
scoreboard this league actually plays for, whether all ten of them admit it or
not. It is printed below. Read it and weep, or read it and gloat. Those are the
only two options this league has ever offered.</p>
